# Prisma migrations are NEVER line-ending-normalized.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows stores LF (as this repo already does) but then
# checks out CRLF. That is harmless for source files and actively dangerous for
# migrations: Prisma computes the `checksum` column of `_prisma_migrations` from
# the *bytes of the migration file as it exists on disk*, so a CRLF checkout
# hashes differently from the LF that was applied. `prisma migrate status` then
# reports drift on an already-applied migration that is, content for content,
# identical — and the natural-looking response ("resolve --applied", or worse
# "migrate reset" to make it agree) would corrupt history to fix a line ending.
#
# Verified for this repository: the blob git stores for
# `20261008120000_engineering_study_types` is `f3d31d7c` (LF), which is what
# production recorded, while a fresh checkout of it was CRLF.
#
# `text` + `eol=lf` pins the worktree to LF as well as the object store, so the
# file a developer and a CI runner see is byte-identical to the one that was
# applied. Only Prisma migrations are scoped here: changing the rule globally
# would re-normalise every text file in the repo and produce an unrelated diff.

prisma/migrations/**/*.sql text eol=lf
